\title[GCD moments of a semiprime]{GCD Moments of a Semiprime:\\ a Closed Trace-Witness Family}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{14 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
For a positive integer $n$ and an exponent $k \ge 1$ define the $k$-th gcd moment
$M_k(n) = \sum_{x=0}^{n-1}\gcd(n,x)^k$. We analyse this family completely, first on semiprime
moduli $N = pq$ with $p \ne q$ prime and then on arbitrary moduli. The moment is the Dirichlet
convolution $\Id_k * \varphi$, giving the divisor form $M_k(n) = \sum_{d \mid n} d^k\varphi(n/d)$
and multiplicativity. On a semiprime, with trace $s = p+q$ and Newton power sums
$P_j = p^j+q^j$ generated by $P_{j+2} = sP_{j+1} - NP_j$, we prove the closed form
$M_k(N) = N^k + NP_{k-1} - P_k + N - s + 1$, an integer polynomial in the public modulus and the
trace alone; in particular $M_1 = 4N - 2s + 1$, so the first moment recovers the trace and hence
the factorisation, while every higher moment is an explicit function of $N$ and $M_1$. We prove
the two-sided variance estimate $N^{2k-1} - 16N^{2k-2} \le \Var(\gcd(N,U)^k) \le 4N^{2k-1}$ for
uniform $U$, together with the separation $\tfrac{N^2}{8}\Var_1 \le \Var_2$ for $N \ge 32$, and
the exact witness density $\#\{x < N : \gcd(N,x) \ne 1\} = p+q-1$. Recasting $M_k$ through its
local factors $L_k(a) = a^k+a-1$ we study the prediction
$E_k(a_1,\dots,a_r) = \prod_i L_k(a_i)$ of an arbitrary factorisation: splitting a part strictly
raises it, the trivial factorisation is the unique minimiser, the prime factorisation the unique
maximiser, and consequently $n^k+n-1 \le M_k(n) \le \Pi_k(n)$ with left equality exactly at
primes and right equality exactly at squarefree moduli. We classify all second-moment collisions
of two-part factorisations (exactly two moduli, $28 = 2\cdot 14 = 4\cdot 7$ and
$36 = 2\cdot 18 = 3\cdot 12$), prove unconditional strict spread monotonicity at every $k \ge 3$
with a sharp exceptional set of seven quadruples, and deduce that for every $k \ge 1$ the
observed moment of a distinct-prime semiprime is matched by exactly one candidate factorisation.
Beyond semiprimes, the prediction separates all factorisations when $\bigOmega(n) \le 2$ (any
$k$) and when $\bigOmega(n) \le 3$ at $k=1$ and at every $k \ge 3$; both bounds are sharp.
\end{abstract}

